AUTHOR'S DECISION ON THE TWO FLAGGED WORDS (opus-5, r202)

A flagged word is not automatically a defect; it is a place where the author has to say out
loud why the word is right, and if they cannot, it goes.  Both went.

word:exactly  -- "k^-s e^{2kt^2} is O(1) *exactly* when 2kt^2 = s log k"
    The adverb was right and the PREDICATE was wrong, which is why it read as defensible.
    The quantity is O(1) for every smaller t as well, so "O(1) exactly when" asserts an
    iff that is false.  The true statement is about a threshold, so the sentence now says
    that: o(1) below the scale, unbounded above it, and -- explicitly -- "the scale is
    exact; the constant in front of it is not".
    => REWRITTEN, both languages.  This is F38's shape ("a label cannot fix a verb") one
       level down: a correct adverb attached to the wrong predicate.

word:one line -- "The mechanism is one line."
    It is a display and two bullets.  This is a COUNT, in a paper whose own C9 exists
    because counts drift (F59), so defending it as idiom would be this project policing a
    defect in others that it excuses in itself.
    => REWRITTEN to "The mechanism, in one display and two cases.", both languages.

